<question type="description"><name><text>Module 2 · Lesson 8 · Section 2: The Semitic Prefix Conjugation — Overview</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<h3>Section 2: The Semitic Prefix Conjugation — Overview</h3>
<p>We have learned that the Participle handles the <em>Present</em>
(ongoing action) and the <em>Perfect</em> (Suffix Conjugation) handles
the <em>Past</em> (completed action).</p>
<p>How does Syriac talk about the Future or expressing wishes/commands?
It uses the <strong>Imperfect Tense</strong>, often called the “Prefix
Conjugation.”</p>
<p><strong>Cross-Linguistic Comparison: The Imperfect</strong></p>
<p>In all Semitic languages, the Imperfect adds prefixes (<em>n-, t-,
ʾ-, y-</em>, etc.) to the root to indicate the person, while suffixes
are used to indicate plural number.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hebrew Qal Imperfect</strong>: יִכְתֹּב (<em>yiktōb</em> — he
will write), אֶכְתֹּב (<em>ʾektōb</em> — I will write).</li>
<li><strong>Arabic Form I Imperfect</strong>: يَكْتُبُ (<em>yaktubu</em> —
he writes/will write), أَكْتُبُ (<em>ʾaktubu</em> — I write).</li>
<li><strong>Syriac Peal Imperfect</strong>: ܢܟܬܘܒ (<em>nektūb</em> — he
will write), ܐܟܬܘܒ (<em>ʾektūb</em> — I will write).</li>
</ul>
<p><em>Crucial Note on Syriac vs. Hebrew/Arabic:</em> In Hebrew and
Arabic, the 3rd person masculine prefix is <strong>y-</strong>
(<em>ya-</em> or <em>yi-</em>). In standard Syriac, the 3rd person
prefix shifted to <strong>n-</strong> (<em>ne-</em>).</p>
<hr />

]]></text></questiontext></question>
<question type="description"><name><text>Module 2 · Lesson 8 · Section 3: Morphology and Syntax of the Imperfect</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<h3>Section 3: Morphology and Syntax of the Imperfect</h3>
<p><strong>1. Forming the Peal Imperfect</strong></p>
<p>For a strong root like <em>k-t-b</em>, the base vocalization of the
imperfect shifts to an <em>ū</em> (Waw) theme vowel between the second
and third consonant: <em>-ktūb</em>. We then add the person-marking
prefixes:</p>
<ul>
<li><em>n-</em> (He / They m) <span class="math inline">→</span>
<em>n-ek-tūb</em> <span class="math inline">→</span>
<strong>nektūb</strong> (He will write).</li>
<li><em>t-</em> (You / She / They f) <span class="math inline">→</span>
<strong>tektūb</strong> (You will write / She will write).</li>
<li><em>ʾ-</em> (I) <span class="math inline">→</span>
<strong>ʾektūb</strong> (I will write).</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>2. Syntax: The Simple Future</strong></p>
<p>By itself, the Imperfect functions as a simple future tense.</p>
<ul>
<li><em>Nektūb Yūḥannan meltā</em>. (Yukhannan will write a word).</li>
<li><em>Teḥze tammān malkā</em>. (You will see a king there).</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>3. Syntax: The Subjunctive / Purpose (<code>d-</code> +
Imperfect)</strong></p>
<p>Syriac uses the Imperfect heavily for purpose clauses, wishes, and
commands. It accomplishes this by attaching the relative particle
<code>d-</code> directly to the Imperfect verb. This corresponds to the
Greek subjunctive with ἵνα (<em>hina</em>).</p>
<ul>
<li><em>ʾEtā <strong>d-neḥze</strong></em> = He came <strong>in order
that he might see</strong>.</li>
<li><em>ʾĀmer-nā d-tektūb</em> = I say <strong>that you should
write</strong>.</li>
</ul>
<hr />

]]></text></questiontext></question>
<question type="multichoice"><name><text>Module 2 · Lesson 8 · Check 6</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[What is the Syriac imperfect (prefix conjugation) mainly used for?]]></text></questiontext><generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[]]></text></generalfeedback><single>true</single><shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers><answer fraction="100" format="html"><text><![CDATA[The future, and the subjunctive in purpose clauses after <i>d-</i>]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[Correct.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[The completed past]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[That is the perfect.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[The present]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[The present uses the participle.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[Commands only]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[Commands use the imperative.]]></text></feedback></answer></question>
<question type="description"><name><text>Module 2 · Lesson 8 · Section 4: Imperfect Tense Paradigms (Peal, Paael, Aphel)</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<h3>Section 4: Imperfect Tense Paradigms (Peal, Paael, Aphel)</h3>
<p><strong>1. The Peal Imperfect Conjugation (Root
<code>q-ṭ-l</code>)</strong></p>
<p>In the Peal, the prefix has an <em>e</em> vowel in modern
pronunciation (or <em>i</em> historically), and the theme vowel is
usually <em>ū</em> (or <em>a</em> for gutturals).</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="text-align: left;">Person</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">Estrangela</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">Translit</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">Translation</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">Prefix</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">Suffix</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>3 m.sg.</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܢܩܛܘܠ</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">neqṭūl</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">he will kill</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">n-</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">(none)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>3 f.sg.</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܬܩܛܘܠ</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">teqṭūl</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">she will kill</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">t-</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">(none)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>2 m.sg.</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܬܩܛܘܠ</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">teqṭūl</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">you (m) will kill</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">t-</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">(none)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>2 f.sg.</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܬܩܛܠܝܢ</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">teqṭlīn</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">you (f) will kill</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">t-</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">-īn</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>1 c.sg.</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܐܩܛܘܠ</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ʾeqṭūl</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">I will kill</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ʾ-</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">(none)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>3 m.pl.</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܢܩܛܠܘܢ</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">neqṭlūn</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">they (m) will kill</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">n-</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">-ūn</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>3 f.pl.</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܢܩܛܠܢ</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">neqṭlān</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">they (f) will kill</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">n-</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">-ān</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>1 c.pl.</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܢܩܛܘܠ</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">neqṭūl</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">we will kill</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">n-</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">(none)</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><em>Warning: The forms for “He will kill” (</em>neqṭūl<em>) and “We
will kill” (</em>neqṭūl<em>) are identical in spelling and
pronunciation! Context almost always makes it obvious.</em></p>
<p><strong>2. The Paael Imperfect (Root <code>q-d-š</code>, to
sanctify)</strong></p>
<p>In the Paael the prefix has only a reduced vowel (<em>nqaddeš</em>),
and the middle root letter is doubled.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="text-align: left;">Person</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">Estrangela</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">Translit</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">Translation</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>3 m.sg.</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܢܩܕܫ</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">nqaddeš</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">he will sanctify</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>1 c.sg.</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܐܩܕܫ</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ʾqaddeš</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">I will sanctify</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>3 m.pl.</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܢܩܕܫܘܢ</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">nqaddšūn</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">they will sanctify</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>1 c.pl.</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܢܩܕܫ</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">nqaddeš</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">we will sanctify</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><strong>3. The Aphel Imperfect (Root <code>n-p-q</code>, Aphel =
<code>ʾappeq</code> to bring out)</strong></p>
<p>In the Aphel, the original <em>ʾa-</em> prefix of the perfect drops
out, and the imperfect prefix takes its place with an <em>a</em> vowel.
The theme vowel is <em>e</em>.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="text-align: left;">Person</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">Estrangela</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">Translit</th>
<th style="text-align: left;">Translation</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>3 m.sg.</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܢܦܩ</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">nappeq</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">he will bring out</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>1 c.sg.</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܐܦܩ</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ʾappeq</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">I will bring out</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: left;"><strong>3 m.pl.</strong></td>
<td style="text-align: left;">ܢܦܩܘܢ</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">nappqūn</td>
<td style="text-align: left;">they will bring out</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
]]></text></questiontext></question>
